  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Tuesday...\n\nSatellite imagery shows a trof digging thru WA/OR, while the weak\nnorthern split portion of the trof skirts across southern Canada.\nWe won't see precip over the next 24 hours, but the wave to our\nnorth will allow for a reinforcing push of cooler Canadian air\nstarting with a frontal passage early today (shifting winds from \nS-SW to NW-N). Surface winds will veer to the NE-E late today and\ntonight. Something to watch is a risk of fog in our east late\ntonight into early Thursday.\n\nReturn flow tonight and Thursday will drive pwats back up to\nanomalous levels, in excess of an inch over the east. The high\nmoisture content will remain in place into the weekend as we await\nthe slow-moving low to our west. Chance of showers will increase\nThursday in two areas, our west per the approaching low, and in\nfar southeast MT as a weak monsoonal wave lifts out of the SW\nCONUS and thru eastern WY. Periods of showers and some embedded\nt-storms are expected Friday and Saturday as the low tracks in\nsome fashion through ID/WY/MT, eventually exiting to the east\nSunday. Weak flow aloft through this period of time is a reason\nfor model uncertainty, and the ensemble ranges in potential QPF \nshow very high spread. Generally speaking, from Friday thru \nSaturday night, there is a greater than 50% chance of a half inch \nof rain across most of the forecast area. Highest probs for an \ninch of rain are in our northeast, which makes sense given the \nhigh pwats to our east and some potential for dry slotting \nemerging from northern WY. This will be a seasonably warm system \nwith above normal heights persisting. Look for snow levels to stay\nabove 10kft even under the upper low itself on Sunday. Regarding \nsevere potential, bulk shear will remain weak but if there is an \nopportunity for strong storms it seems to be Friday when \ninstability will be highest and low level winds remain easterly. \nSomething to watch.\n\nLatest models have trended a bit slower with the departure of the\nlow, and thus showers will likely linger in our east into Sunday.\nEnsemble consensus is for ridging to reestablish itself early next\nweek leading to a warmer and dry Monday/Tuesday...but it is also\npossible that the ridge begins to break down again by the middle\nof next week. High uncertainty here.\n\nCooler than normal temps today should warm to near normal Thursday\nand a little above normal Friday (the warmest day of the week w/ \nmid 70s to lower 80s). There will be some magnitude of cooling for\nthe weekend (mid 60s to mid 70s) before temps rise back to the \nmid 70s to lower 80s Monday/Tuesday. Overall nothing extreme\neither way.\n\nJKL",
